Nethack is per default installed in a _system_ directory (/usr/games).

Also supporting programs like recover expect the default directory, or
alternatively, allow to specify a directory different to the default by
the -d option. I consider it to be very consistent if the loghack perl
program behaves accordingly. It is as I would expect it to behave; the
principle of "least surprise" applies here (for me).

> or, the location from which the application is installed. <snip>

A possible extension could be implementing a search path sequence, say
1. an explicit option overrides everything
2. else inspect the NETHACKDIR environment variable
3. else look in the default system installation directory
4. else look into CWD (current working directory)
5. else look into HOME directory
But that's all far overkill for the nice perl program that Markus has
contributed. As I said, for me it behaves as I'd expect.

